  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1149 PM MDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nWe are still expecting a few showers, and maybe a thunderstorm\ntoday closer to the Utah border, throughout the day. The low\ncontinues to track farther south, and it may end up far enough\nsouth that our forecast for isolated coverage is a stretch.\nExpect little to no rain, and a low potential for gusts over 35\nmph. Otherwise, it remains dry and warm until the upcoming\nweekend. The flow remains more westerly, perhaps even a bit\nwest-northwest until we get to Friday. This will still pull in\nthe dry air, but temperatures will be just a bit lower. That\nstill means highs will run 5-15 degrees ABOVE AVERAGE. Highs for\nthe valleys are expected to be in the mid 70s to low 80s, with\nsome mid 80s sprinkled in here and there. Winds aren't going to\nstrong until we get to Friday at the moment. As the low begins\nto drop into the Pacific Northwest at that point, that is we \nwill see winds starting to ramp up.  \n\nThe weather pattern over Idaho and much of the West drastically\nchanges over the weekend. Both the GFS and ECMWF, including\ntheir ensemble and AI versions of each model, show low pressure\nclosing off or in the process of doing so by Sunday afternoon.\nShowers and thunderstorms quickly ramp up across the area. Most\nversions of the forecast have the low just to our west and\nsoutherly flow, which would hold temperatures up a bit...but\nstill much, much cooler. Our Blend of Models has highs in the\n50s/low 60s for the valleys Sunday. That seems reasonable,\nalthough that may be on the high side of things. If we maximize\ncloud cover and the low shifts more overhead, which is also\nshown as a solution for Sunday, highs may not make it out of \nthe 40s down low and 20s and 30s up in the mountains. The GFS \nand ECMWF ensemble forecasts show a 20-50% chance for snow in \nthe central mountains and eastern highlands Sunday afternoon. \nThe the trend in the GFS and ECMWF is to swing the low a bit \nmore to our southwest, then across Idaho and Utah Tuesday. The \nensemble forecasts, especially related to ECMWF, are cold enough\nfor some snow at least falling across the Snake Plain and Magic\nValley Monday into Tuesday morning. While certainly not a\nguarantee by any stretch, it is something to understand COULD\nhappen. The knowns are that it will be cooler and we should some\ndecent precipitation falling based on current trends. Stay\ntuned.",
